--- body ---
## Context

Child 4 of tracker `tickets/0240`, which owns the rationale.

Same corpus, same probes, same driver as the CPU arm — the tracker is explicit
that the two arms share everything but the hardware. What differs is that fp16
is a live rung here and unloadable there, so the arms do not have the same shape
and the report must say so rather than presenting one table.

`gpu-feasibility.json`'s A4000 figures were derived from PyTorch run logs on
another machine: upper bounds by projection, not measurements in the shipped
runtime. This ticket replaces them with real ONNX numbers.

**The load-bearing unexecuted claim.** That `auto` selects a GPU provider is
today read from `src/backends/onnx.js`, not observed. Nobody has run it. That
single observation is the reason this arm exists at all, and it is the first
thing to record.

## Relevant files

- `tickets/0240-...erg` — the tracker
- `tickets/0220-...erg` — why device is `auto` and never a knob; its reading of
  `deviceToExecutionProviders` is the claim under test here
- the harness from `tickets/0262`
- `bench/results/0025-x1-recall/gpu-feasibility.json` — the projections being
  replaced
- `bench/gpu_feasibility.py`

## Actions

1. Reach padme and stand the harness up there. Record the host, its GPU, its
   driver and CUDA version alongside the results.
2. **Observe what `auto` resolves to.** Record the execution provider actually
   selected, from the runtime, not from source.
3. Run the same cells as the CPU arm: fp32, fp16, q8, uint8 per candidate. fp16
   is expected to load here; if it does not, that is a result.
4. Probe the tracker's open engine questions on real hardware: whether `q4f16`
   fails for the same reason as fp16 on CPU or a different one, and whether the
   WebGPU path is usable from Node at all.
5. Record the state of upstream `huggingface/transformers.js#945` at run time.
   If it lands, the meaning of the default changes and every CPU baseline needs
   re-reading.
6. Report the CPU and GPU arms as two shapes, not one merged table.

## Test

- The harness's own tests (0262) gate the run.
- The device recorded in each result file is the device selected, and a test
  asserts it is read from the runtime rather than echoed from the request.

## Verification

- [ ] `auto` on padme is **observed** to select a GPU provider, with the provider
      named — the claim is currently unexecuted
- [ ] Each result file names its four engine versions and the host
- [ ] fp16 recorded as loadable or not, with the error if not

## Invariants

- Same corpus, same probes, same driver as the CPU arm
- Warm only; median over repeats with spread
- No projected figure is presented beside a measured one without saying which

## Exit criteria

- [ ] GPU arm complete for every surviving candidate at fp32, fp16, q8, uint8
- [ ] The provider `auto` selected is recorded from observation
- [ ] `gpu-feasibility.json`'s projections are superseded in place, with the
      supersession stated where they are cited
